[38;2;166;226;46m+[m[38;2;166;226;46m## Frame starvation through a PTY — 2026-11-20[m
[38;2;166;226;46m+[m
[38;2;166;226;46m+[m[38;2;166;226;46m`flood` (*Pacing the write path*) has no PTY, and there the unpaced case drew 37[m
[38;2;166;226;46m+[m[38;2;166;226;46mframes a second and was never freezing. Through a real PTY it does freeze. The[m
[38;2;166;226;46m+[m[38;2;166;226;46mexample app (`e2e_bench.dart` with `XB_PROF`, release, `flutter_pty2` 1.0.0,[m
[38;2;166;226;46m+[m[38;2;166;226;46mview on) draws **1-8 frames during a whole 64 MiB payload**, and the longest[m
[38;2;166;226;46m+[m[38;2;166;226;46mgap between two frames is **0.2-2.8 s** — every unpaced run in this section and[m
[38;2;166;226;46m+[m[38;2;166;226;46mthe next two. All tables here: real PTY, 64 MiB per payload, 100x37, Apple M1[m
[38;2;166;226;46m+[m[38;2;166;226;46mPro, display at 75 Hz, five interleaved rounds, medians.[m
[38;2;166;226;46m+[m
[38;2;166;226;46m+[m[38;2;166;226;46m**The cause, measured.** `flutter_pty2` 1.0.0 posts one port message per[m
[38;2;166;226;46m+[m[38;2;166;226;46m`read()`, and a macOS PTY returns about 1 KiB per read: 0.99 KiB per[m
[38;2;166;226;46m+[m[38;2;166;226;46m`terminal.write` call. The messages arrive far faster than they are parsed. The[m
[38;2;166;226;46m+[m[38;2;166;226;46mdriver has handed over all 64 MiB after 0.5-0.8 s, with 5-53 MiB still[m
[38;2;166;226;46m+[m[38;2;166;226;46munparsed at that point (derived), and the UI thread spends the rest of the[m
[38;2;166;226;46m+[m[38;2;166;226;46mpayload draining that backlog. Split where the driver finished writing[m
[38;2;166;226;46m+[m[38;2;166;226;46m(`spike_phase.py`, run 3 below), every unpaced frame falls in the burst; the[m
[38;2;166;226;46m+[m[38;2;166;226;46mtail — 1.8 s on unicode, 2.3 s on cjk — draws none. Peak RSS is up to 106 MiB[m
[38;2;166;226;46m+[m[38;2;166;226;46mabove the same run with `ackRead` (unicode 304 against 198 MiB).[m
[38;2;166;226;46m+[m
