[38;2;166;226;46m+[m[38;2;166;226;46m**Back pressure works, and on 1.0.0 it is expensive.** `ackRead` holds the[m
[38;2;166;226;46m+[m[38;2;166;226;46mdisplay's 75 fps with a longest gap of 14-16 ms and no backlog, at 48-76% less[m
[38;2;166;226;46m+[m[38;2;166;226;46mthroughput (ascii 77.4 → 18.9, unicode 27.7 → 12.8, cjk 22.9 → 11.8): every[m
[38;2;166;226;46m+[m[38;2;166;226;46m~1 KiB chunk waits for its own ack round trip. Acking on arrival recovers part[m
[38;2;166;226;46m+[m[38;2;166;226;46mof it and nothing else changes.[m
[38;2;166;226;46m+[m
[38;2;166;226;46m+[m[38;2;166;226;46m**The shipped `PacedTerminalWriter` did nothing.** Never more than one chunk[m
[38;2;166;226;46m+[m[38;2;166;226;46mqueued (`pendingChunks` max 1), frames and gaps as unpaced. Its budget was per[m
[38;2;166;226;46m+[m[38;2;166;226;46mdrain pass, and a pass ended when the queue was empty: a PTY delivers one chunk[m
[38;2;166;226;46m+[m[38;2;166;226;46mper event-loop turn, so every pass wrote its one chunk and returned, and the[m
[38;2;166;226;46m+[m[38;2;166;226;46mbudget was never reached.[m
[38;2;166;226;46m+[m
[38;2;166;226;46m+[m[38;2;166;226;46m**A budget that spans turns does work.** `paced-span` is a bench-only pacer[m
[38;2;166;226;46m+[m[38;2;166;226;46m(`XB_PACED=2`) that sums the time spent in `Terminal.write` across chunks and[m
[38;2;166;226;46m+[m[38;2;166;226;46mqueues whatever arrives past the budget until after the frame: the longest gap[m
[38;2;166;226;46m+[m[38;2;166;226;46mgoes from 0.3-2.7 s to 24-54 ms, at 13-26% of throughput. An 11 ms budget buys[m
[38;2;166;226;46m+[m[38;2;166;226;46msome throughput back and lengthens the gaps. Both still take everything the PTY[m
[38;2;166;226;46m+[m[38;2;166;226;46mdelivers into their queue — up to 62039 chunks.[m
[38;2;166;226;46m+[m
[38;2;166;226;46m+[m[38;2;166;226;46m**An isolate is smooth and buys no throughput.** `isolate-push`: a worker[m
[38;2;166;226;46m+[m[38;2;166;226;46misolate owns the PTY and a headless `Terminal`, and pushes a 100x37 snapshot of[m
[38;2;166;226;46m+[m[38;2;166;226;46mthe visible grid at most every 13 ms to a mirror `Terminal` that the view[m
[38;2;166;226;46m+[m[38;2;166;226;46mshows. Longest gap 17 ms on every payload, at 88-96% of unpaced throughput in[m
[38;2;166;226;46m+[m[38;2;166;226;46mthat run. No gain, because the stream is sequential and another core parses it[m
[38;2;166;226;46m+[m[38;2;166;226;46mat the same rate, and the backlog is as unbounded as unpaced (20-53 MiB[m
[38;2;166;226;46m+[m[38;2;166;226;46munparsed when the driver finishes). A snapshot is 59.2 KB and takes ~22 us to[m
[38;2;166;226;46m+[m[38;2;166;226;46mbuild in the worker; applying it on the UI thread took 59-67 us in runs 1 and 2[m
[38;2;166;226;46m+[m[38;2;166;226;46mand 87-92 us in run 3, and a snapshot reached the UI a median 0.1-0.2 ms after[m
[38;2;166;226;46m+[m[38;2;166;226;46mit was requested. Run 2's `isolate-push` was smooth but less so — 61.4 / 77 / 26,[m
[38;2;166;226;46m+[m[38;2;166;226;46m24.7 / 192 / 27, 20.6 / 232 / 27 — and the data does not show whether that[m
[38;2;166;226;46m+[m[38;2;166;226;46mbuild differed from run 3's.[m
[38;2;166;226;46m+[m
[38;2;166;226;46m+[m[38;2;166;226;46m**Not pursued.** The spike mirrors only the visible cells. A real[m
[38;2;166;226;46m+[m[38;2;166;226;46mimplementation would have to carry scrollback, combining characters, the[m
[38;2;166;226;46m+[m[38;2;166;226;46mhyperlink table, input, resize, selection and search across the isolate[m
[38;2;166;226;46m+[m[38;2;166;226;46mboundary; answer the callbacks a `Terminal` makes synchronously today (queries[m
