Author: klc <m_kilic@outlook.com.tr>
Date:   Sun Nov 15 23:38:34 2026 +0300

    perf(terminal): stop scanning both buffers for every hyperlink written
    
    Two scans kept the OSC 8 registry bounded, and both were sized for a
    buffer much smaller than the ones in use.
    
    Evicted hyperlink ids were resolved in batches of 128, each batch a scan
    of both buffers. With a link on every line (`ls --hyperlink`) that is a
    scan of the whole scrollback every 128 lines, whatever its size.
    
    At the 4096-link ceiling, every further link pruned the registry before
    giving up - another full scan, which with a scrollback full of live links
    frees nothing, or only the few that scrolled out since the last one. At
    10000 lines that was a scan per line written: 1.8 ms a line.
    
    The batch now grows with the scrollback (a quarter of its lines, between
    128 and half the ceiling), and pruning at the ceiling happens at most
    once per that many link opens. Both scans, and the per-line collection on
    eviction, stop at `BufferLine.writtenLength` instead of walking the blank
    tail of each line.
    
    Ten interleaved rounds, old against new, one link per line, on an idle
    M1 Pro:
    
      10000 lines of scrollback   1790 -> 4.33 us/line
      1000 lines of scrollback    7.24 -> 3.89 us/line
      one live link, plain output
        scrolling past it         0.90 -> 0.75 us/line
    
    Behaviour at the ceiling changes with it. A link opened while the
    registry is full still gets no link, as before, but the registry is now
    re-checked once per interval rather than on every open: links freed by an
    erase are picked up within at most 2048 opens instead of at the next one,
    and with one link per line at 10000 lines the buffer ends up carrying
    about 2100 linked lines where it carried 4096. Raising the ceiling would
    restore that, and is left alone here because it also bounds what hostile
    output can make the terminal hold.
    
