    perf(buffer): do not grow a line to erase cells nothing was written to
    
    `eraseRange` cleared cell by cell through `eraseCell`, which makes sure
    the cell has storage first. So `ESC[K` after a line of text - what
    `grep --color` and compiler diagnostics end every line with - grew the
    line's store from what the text needed to the full width of the window,
    then wrote zeros into cells that already read as zero. That undid the
    born-small store for exactly the output that is kept in scrollback, and
    paid an allocation, a copy and a loop per line to do it.
    
    A line now tracks how far it has been written. Erasing to the default
    background stops there: past it there is nothing to clear and no reason
    to allocate. An erase that reaches the mark lowers it, so repeated erases
    on one line cost nothing either. Erasing with a background colour still
    fills, because those cells have to hold the colour.
    
    Ten interleaved rounds, old against new, 170x50 with 10000 lines of
    scrollback, on an idle M1 Pro:
    
      lines ending in ESC[K     63.5 -> 130 MiB/s, store 29.3 -> 14.6 MB
      grep --color shaped         12 ->  39 MiB/s
      30-column lines + ESC[K     22 ->  58 MiB/s, store 29.3 ->  9.8 MB
      clear screen and redraw     67 -> 108 MiB/s
    
    The mark is one comparison in `_ensureCapacity`, which every store goes
    through. `bin/parse_bench.dart` is unchanged in all six workloads. The
    one place it may show is wide characters, which call it per cell: CJK
    measured 9.3 -> 9.1 M chars/s, ranges touching at the edge.
    
    `BufferLine.writtenLength` exposes the mark for scans that only care
    about cells holding something.
    
