    docs(media): retake the screenshots, reproducibly
    
    The four images in the README came from the upstream project and showed a
    2020 Linux desktop and a 2020 macOS window with a DEBUG banner on it. None of
    them was a frame this package had ever painted.
    
    They are now produced in two steps, both of which can be re-run.
    script/screenshots/record.py drives each program in a real PTY pinned to
    100x30 and saves every byte it printed; example/lib/screenshot.dart replays a
    recording into a Terminal, paints one frame of a real TerminalView into a
    RepaintBoundary, and writes the PNG. Only the recording step needs the
    programs installed, and the rendering step has no PTY and no keystroke
    injection, so the same recording gives the same image.
    
    The scenes were chosen to show what the package claims: the shell scene
    carries CJK, emoji, combining marks and Cyrillic, which is the write path the
    benchmarks are about; htop is block elements on the procedural glyph path; vim
    is a full-screen alternate-screen redraw. demo-dialog.png is replaced by
    demo-search.png, where Terminal.search finds the matches and the controller
    renders them - the highlights are xterm3's, not the recorded program's, so
    that scene has no recording of its own.
    
    example-ssh.png is removed rather than retaken. Reproducing it needs an SSH
    server, this machine has none listening, and photographing a local shell to
    pass it off as a remote session would be a lie in a README about measurement.
    The link to example/lib/ssh.dart stays.
    
    script/screenshots/README.md carries the warning that matters: record.py
    captures whatever the machine prints, and the htop scene is a real process
    list with real paths in it.
    
