Author: klc <m_kilic@outlook.com.tr>
Date:   Thu Sep 3 20:58:57 2026 +0300

    bench: measure the three packages on one harness
    
    The tables in BENCHMARKS.md compared this tree against its own earlier
    commits, which answers what a phase did but not what the package is worth
    against the line it came from. script/cross_bench/ is its own package so it
    can depend on xterm, xterm2 and xterm3 at once; each is driven through its
    headless core.dart, and all three expose the same Terminal/resize/write, so
    nothing per-package sits in the measured path.
    
    Throughput at 170x50, 32 MiB per workload, medians of three interleaved
    rounds, against xterm 4.0.0 / xterm2 5.2.0: plain ASCII 15.8 / 86.6 / 113.4
    MiB/s, Cyrillic 14.8 / 5.5 / 90.4, alt-screen 19.2 / 133.2 / 139.1. Scrollback
    residency for 10000 lines: 85.8 / 79.3 / 52.8 MB. Resize reflow over the same
    scrollback: 10.6 / 4.7 / 4.6 ms.
    
    Two rows are recorded because they are not wins. SGR output is 6% behind
    xterm2 — the known price of born-small line storage, which is what buys the
    residency row. Reflow is level with xterm2; the 2.3x there is against xterm.
    
    Memory is measured after driving the collector through several generations
    and taking the low-water mark. Dart exposes no explicit collection, and the
    first attempt reported the heap with its garbage still in it: 88 / 105 / 81 MB,
    which had xterm2 as the worst of the three rather than the middle.
    
