    chore: release 6.3.0
    
    Minor rather than patch: no API is added or removed, but a public class
    changes behaviour. BufferLine no longer allocates its cell storage at the
    terminal's width - the first write sizes it - so the deprecated
    `data` accessor reports a different length than it used to and can report
    an empty list, and the package's memory and throughput profile moves with
    it.
    
    The one change that reaches the published archive is that line-storage
    work. Everything else unreleased since 6.2.0 stays out of the changelog
    because it does not ship: the CSI parameter census and the scrollback
    retention probe live in script/, which .pubignore excludes; the fuzz
    regression prose, the RENDER_PLAN and BENCHMARKS updates and the CI
    formatting fix touch nothing a consumer downloads.
    
    SGR-heavy output is 13% slower and the changelog says so in those words.
    It buys ASCII +18%, Cyrillic +19%, UTF-8 +24% and a scrollback that
    retains a half to a third of what it did, and the two other growth
    policies that were measured came out worse.
    
    Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
