[38;2;253;151;31mcommit c2c90a731dac75165c2150839148d051d29996c8[m
Author: klc <m_kilic@outlook.com.tr>
Date:   Sun Aug 30 18:21:49 2026 +0300

    docs(plan): retire phase 7's premise, it was reading the wrong column
    
    Phase 7 proposed a fast-forward mode - skip scrollback writes, reflow and
    anchor maintenance while the backlog is deep - and put its ceiling at
    +63% on ascii and +64% on cyrillic. That ceiling came from parse_bench's
    `scrollback` column, and the column does not measure what the mode would
    skip.
    
    `scrollback` runs at `maxLines: 0`, which the buffer widens to
    `max(maxLines, viewHeight)` = 50. So the two runs differ in exactly one
    thing: how many lines stay alive, 50 against 10000.
    `IndexAwareCircularBuffer.push` does the same work either way - and less
    at depth 10000, where the ring is not full yet and neither `onEvict` nor
    the hyperlink pruning behind it runs at all. It does less work and is
    slower, which places the whole gap in the live set rather than in
    anything a mode could skip.
    
    A fast-forward that keeps history therefore collects none of it. The gap
    is what retaining scrollback costs; collecting it means dropping lines,
    which is a data-loss decision and not a performance mode.
    
    What can attack it is the size of each retained line, which is phase 6.1.
    Measured on top of it, the same gap is already smaller: ascii 41% -> 32%,
    utf8 30% -> 15%, cyrillic 40% -> 30%.
    
