Author: klc <m_kilic@outlook.com.tr>
Date:   Sun Aug 30 18:04:41 2026 +0300

    perf(buffer): size a line's store from its text, not from the viewport
    
    A BufferLine allocated its cell store at birth, sized to the viewport, so
    a 60-column line of output retained the same 3072 bytes as a 170-column
    one. Phase 6 measured what that costs in time - the garbage collector
    promoting each store into old space, a copy proportional to the store
    rather than to the text in it - and left "born small" as the open
    candidate with a 1.2-3.0x ceiling.
    
    That ceiling came from a probe that modelled lines born at the right
    size. A real Buffer cannot do that: the line is created blank, before
    anything knows how wide it will be written. Three shapes that try to
    approximate it all measured negative - born at 64 and grown in 32-cell
    steps (-11% to -22%), born at 64 and jumped to full width (-12% to
    -17%), born at 128 (-4% to -30%). Counters show why: any line that grows
    pays an extra allocation and a copy, and its surviving store is no
    smaller than before, so it pays the cost without collecting the win.
    `ascii-long-lines` is the clearest case - it grows to 192 cells, exactly
    what it used to be born with.
    
    This is the shape those counters pointed at instead. A line is born
    holding a shared empty list, so scrolling allocates nothing per line, and
    the *first write* sizes the store to what it is about to write. A line
    filled in one run - what scrolling output looks like - gets exactly one
    store, sized to its text. A line that later outgrows it jumps straight to
    its full length rather than climbing in steps.
    
    Five interleaved rounds of `bin/parse_bench.dart`: ascii +18%, cyrillic
    +19%, utf8 +24%, altscreen flat, ascii-long-lines -3%, sgr -13%. The
    capacity accounting on its own - the growth check on writes, the bounds
    check on reads, with allocation unchanged - measured 0 to -1.5%, so what
    these numbers move is the allocation shape and not the checks.
    
    `sgr` is the one real regression and its shape is known: the line is
    filled by six separate short runs, so the first write sizes the store to
    64 and the sixth jumps it to 192 - two allocations and a copy. Growing in
    32-cell steps made that -22% instead; nothing gets it to one allocation,
    because the first write cannot know where the line will end.
    
    Memory, which was phase 6's actual claim, lands where it predicted. At a
    170-column viewport with 10000 lines of scrollback: 90-column output
    29.3 MB -> 14.6 MB, 60-column output 29.3 MB -> 9.8 MB, full-width output
    unchanged. `script/scrollback_retained_probe.dart` measures it.
    
