Author: klc <m_kilic@outlook.com.tr>
Date:   Sun Aug 30 16:46:20 2026 +0300

    perf(parser): close CSI bulk scanning by counting, not benchmarking
    
    Phase 5.4 rejected CSI parameter bulk scanning twice, both times on
    setup cost: four and three separate `_advancePastConsumedBlocks()` calls
    per digit run. That left a third shape unmeasured — putting the scan
    inside `ByteConsumer` so the setup collapses to one — and the baseline
    had since moved to 6.2.0, so it was worth one more measurement.
    
    Measured over six interleaved rounds. `sgr parser` breaks even (value
    sets overlap); `altscreen parser` regresses 2.8% with disjoint value
    sets. Better than 5.4's 12% and 8.3%, still below the baseline. The
    implementation is not committed.
    
    What is committed is why, and it is arithmetic rather than a third
    negative benchmark. A bulk scanner pays its setup once per parameter and
    earns it back once per digit past the first, since the first digit is
    already in hand from the `consume()` that reached the digit branch. On
    `altscreen` that means 300 calls to remove 191 — 53% of its parameters
    are a single digit, so half the calls take nothing. On `sgr` the
    parameters are longer (42.9% two-digit, 19.0% three-digit) and it breaks
    even. Same ordering the benchmark gave, without running it.
    
    The ceiling is small either way: CSI digits are 5.1% of the `altscreen`
    stream, and `consume()` has been a single-comparison fast path since
    phase 5.2. Parameters stay 1-3 digits, so no implementation shape wins
    here. The one remaining shape — scanning per sequence rather than per
    parameter — would have to duplicate separator, `_maxCsiParams` and
    intermediate handling inside the scan loop for that same 5.1% ceiling,
    and is not opened.
    
    `script/csi_param_census.dart` is the counter, alongside the existing
    `scrollback_cost_probe` and `trim_variant_probe`.
    
