Author: klc <m_kilic@outlook.com.tr>
Date:   Sat Aug 29 02:08:50 2026 +0300

    docs: find where the scrollback cost is, and reject trimming
    
    Phase 6 opened as "fast-forward under flood". Measuring first reframed it:
    the cost that idea was aimed at has a source, and a lossless attack on it
    that needs no API change at all. The flood idea drops to Phase 7.
    
    `parse_bench`'s `scrollback` column runs with `maxLines: 0`, which `Buffer`
    widens to `max(maxLines, viewHeight)` — same ring size per scroll, same
    allocation, same eviction bookkeeping. The only variable is how many lines
    stay alive. `script/scrollback_cost_probe.dart` shows that is the whole
    story: a line writing 30 cells costs 151 ns with 50 alive and 561 ns with
    10000 alive. It is the collector promoting each line's 3072-byte backing
    store, at a copy proportional to the store and not to the text in it.
    
    So the lever is line size — the half of Phase 5's closing line nobody had
    tried. Allocated at `_calcCapacity` of the width it writes, at 10000 deep:
    561 → 180 ns at 30 cells, 629 → 345 at 60, 767 → 590 at 120. Scrollback's
    resident cost would go from 30 MB to roughly 10-15.
    
    The obvious form of that does not work. Trimming a line as it leaves the
    viewport is the version with the narrow blast radius — no write-path change,
    the painter already drives off `line.length`, `getText` skips zero code
    points so its output is unchanged, and `Buffer.resize` already grows lines
    back. `script/trim_variant_probe.dart` puts it 9-30% behind doing nothing in
    every cell measured. It spends an allocation and a copy of the small store
    to save promoting the large one, and those are the same size. The saving is
    in never allocating the large store, not in handing it back.
    
    Left open, not implemented: a line born at the 64-cell floor and grown from
    inside `BufferLine`'s write methods, which keeps
    `_data.length == _calcCapacity(_length) * _cellSize` and so needs no read
    guards anywhere. Ceiling 1.2-3.0x; its cost on the per-code-point write path
    is unmeasured, and `altscreen` may lose.
    
    No `lib/` change.
    
