Author: klc <m_kilic@outlook.com.tr>
Date:   Sat Aug 29 01:40:25 2026 +0300

    docs: reject line pooling a second time, before building it
    
    Phase 5.1 ranked pooling with a bounded clear as the top candidate and also
    recorded that a second attempt must measure the root cause first, because
    whether a partial clear can pay depends on it. Measured with
    `script/line_reuse_probe.dart`, which is pure `Uint32List` with no Flutter
    and no xterm3, and the answer is no. Nothing was built.
    
    Per 3072-byte line: allocating costs 92 ns, which is 0.12 ns per word.
    An indexed store loop costs 0.475 and `fillRange` costs 1.6. Allocating is
    four times cheaper per word than the cheapest thing that actually writes the
    words, so it is not writing them.
    
    Pooling therefore wins only at full width, where nothing is left to clear
    and what it wins is exactly the 92 ns of allocation. At 60 written cells of
    192 it loses, 199 ns against 242. The prediction that a bounded clear pays
    on short shell lines came out backwards: a short line leaves a long tail to
    clear, because the line under it was wider. Pool depth does not matter.
    
    Two corrections this run forced, both mine and both left in place as the
    record of a wrong turn:
    
    - A 170-column line is 3072 bytes, not 680. `_calcCapacity` rounds 170 up to
      192 cells at four words each. BENCHMARKS.md had it right the first time.
    - Phase 5's root cause was correct, and Phase 5.1's note calling it a
      probably-wrong hypothesis was itself wrong, resting on that bad size.
    
    One finding with no application here: `fillRange` costs 3.4x an indexed
    store loop, so it does not lower to a memset. `eraseRange` already clears
    cell by cell and the only other `fillRange` calls in `lib/` are one-time
    table setup — recorded so nobody puts one on a hot path later.
    
