    docs: re-baseline the write path after the harness fix
    
    The `parser` column and every `buffer%` derived from it were measured
    through a `noSuchMethod` no-op handler, so two of Phase 5's three readings
    were reading a harness artifact. Re-measured, three runs each, old and new
    side by side.
    
    The parser column was understated by 10-30%. `sgr`'s buffer share goes from
    23% to 36% and `altscreen`'s from 26% to 39%, so "SGR is 77% parser" was
    really 64%. `sgr` is still the most parser-bound workload and still has the
    slowest parser column by a wide margin. The control columns - `full`,
    `scrollback`, `no-grapheme` - never touched the defect and did not move,
    which is what makes the rest of the comparison trustworthy.
    
    Two things recorded without new measurement, both derived from tables Phase
    5 already had:
    
    - 63-93% of the scrollback penalty is line allocation. Phase 5's open
      question about GC promotion is largely answered, and the rejected pooling
      experiment is the evidence: the idea was right, the clearing strategy was
      not.
    - Phase 5's "root cause" for why clearing beat allocating is a hypothesis,
      not a finding. A 170-column line is 680 bytes, which is a new-space
      bump-pointer allocation Dart zeroes explicitly, not an OS-zeroed page.
      Cache locality and whether `fillRange` lowers to a memset are likelier.
      Which one holds decides whether a bounded clear can pay, so a second
      attempt has to measure it first.
    
    Candidate order revised accordingly: line pooling with a bounded clear
    first (highest measured ceiling), then the `ByteConsumer.consume()` ASCII
    fast path (cheap, and it prescreens the third), then CSI bulk scanning
    (demoted, and its ~19 ns/char is an upper bound that still includes CSI
    handler dispatch, not a measurement of `consume()`).
    
    Also recorded as a Phase 6 candidate: fast-forwarding under flood. The
    field symptom does not close with write throughput - production runs
    40-90 MiB/s sustained against 77 MiB/s of `sgr` write, with an unbounded
    queue - so a 30% faster write path delays the freeze rather than removing
    it.
    
