Author: klc <m_kilic@outlook.com.tr>
Date:   Sat Aug 29 01:19:32 2026 +0300

    perf(bench): stop charging the parser for noSuchMethod
    
    `parse_bench`'s no-op handler implemented only `writeChar` and `writeText`
    and let the other 233 `EscapeHandler` members fall through to
    `noSuchMethod`, which allocates an `Invocation` and boxes the arguments on
    every call. Its comment justified this as "escape sequences are rare per
    byte". The workload generators in the same file say otherwise: `ascii`
    calls `carriageReturn` and `lineFeed` about 22,000 times per MiB and `sgr`
    reaches a handler about 50,000 times per MiB.
    
    So the `parser` column was measuring harness dispatch the real `Terminal`
    never pays, and `buffer% = 1 - full/parser` inherited it. Measured cost of
    the artifact: 22-24 ns per zero-argument call, 37-44 ns per call carrying
    arguments.
    
    `NoopEscapeHandler` implements every member explicitly with no
    `noSuchMethod`, so the analyzer now rejects the class whenever
    `EscapeHandler` grows a member the benchmark has not caught up with - a
    missing member becomes a build error instead of a quietly poisoned
    measurement. It is generated by `script/gen_noop_handler.dart` because
    keeping 233 stubs in sync by hand is a chore that gets skipped.
    
    No `lib/` change: `full`, `scrollback` and `no-grapheme` are unmoved
    across three runs, which is the control this change had to pass.
    
