Author: klc <m_kilic@outlook.com.tr>
Date:   Sat Aug 29 01:04:49 2026 +0300

    docs: record the write path measurements and a rejected experiment
    
    Phase 4 of RENDER_PLAN named the flood workload as the one remaining
    behaviour that stresses the frame budget, and left it there. It showed up
    in the field: a Flutter app embedding this package freezes while vtebench
    reports a fast drain, because flutter_pty hands PTY output over a
    ReceivePort that Dart cannot push back on. Terminal.write's own speed is
    the only lever left.
    
    Adds Phase 5 to RENDER_PLAN with the parse_bench decomposition, and
    WRITE_PATH_BRIEF.md as a self-contained handoff for someone picking this
    up cold.
    
    The measurements say the escape parser is not the bottleneck for most
    workloads - plain ASCII parses at 431 MiB/s against a 102 MiB/s full path,
    so three quarters of the time is buffer writes. SGR is the exception, and
    the one that matters in practice: buffer is only 23% there, and real
    terminal output is SGR-heavy. Retaining 10000 lines of scrollback costs
    46-70%.
    
    Also records a rejected experiment, so it is not repeated: pooling evicted
    BufferLines for reuse made things roughly 2x worse. Removing the clear
    shows the pooling itself is a large win (ascii 102 -> 147) and the
    fillRange is what kills it. Allocating a fresh Uint32List is cheaper than
    clearing an existing one, because the VM serves fresh typed data from
    OS-zeroed pages while fillRange really writes a cold, already-faulted
    buffer. The code did not land; this record is what is left of it.
    
