Author: klc <m_kilic@outlook.com.tr>
Date:   Sun Aug 9 10:20:06 2026 +0300

    feat: commit pending IME composition on demand
    
    Input that reaches the terminal from outside the keyboard — a mobile
    extra-keys bar, a paste button — is appended to what the terminal has already
    received. An open IME composition has not been received yet: emission is
    deferred until the composition resolves. So a bar's Tab arrived against an
    empty line while the keyboard still held the `www` the user had typed, and the
    shell completed nothing instead of that word.
    
    TerminalView.commitComposing() emits the composition and reports whether there
    was one, so a caller can flush before sending such a key. resetEditingState()
    stays for the case where the composition really is to be discarded, and now
    says so.
    
    Also fixes the same commit being typed twice under deleteDetection. The guard
    that swallows the IME's delayed echo of an out-of-band commit only existed on
    the non-deleteDetection path, so on Android — where terminals enable
    deleteDetection to keep backspace alive — Enter sent the command and the
    keyboard's delayed commit then replayed the whole word. The guard now covers
    both paths, and is spent on the next editing value so a word typed again later
    is not mistaken for an echo.
    
    Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>

