Author: klc <m_kilic@outlook.com.tr>
Date:   Thu Aug 6 00:27:51 2026 +0300

    perf(buffer): keep Latin text off the grapheme segmenter
    
    `bin/parse_bench.dart`, added here, measures the write path with no Flutter and
    no renderer, so the parser can be told apart from the buffer writes it drives.
    It says the parser is not the bottleneck - plain text parses at 435 MiB/s while
    the full path manages 92 - and that non-ASCII text ran at 15 MiB/s, a sixth of
    ASCII.
    
    `_joinsPreviousGrapheme` shortcut the case where the previous cell and the
    incoming code point are both ASCII. One accented letter defeats it: with `ö` in
    the previous cell, every following character fell through to real grapheme
    segmentation, which allocates two strings and segments both. That is every
    language written in Latin script but English.
    
    Nothing below U+0300 can continue a grapheme cluster - the first combining
    marks are there, and every other class that can extend one (SpacingMark, ZWJ,
    regional indicators, emoji modifiers, Hangul V and T) sits higher - and
    `combining == null` rules out a cluster ending in ZWJ. So the cut is made on
    the code point, and `writeChar` skips both cluster checks for anything below
    it.
    
    15 -> 32 MiB/s on Turkish text, against a 35 MiB/s ceiling with clustering
    disabled outright. What remains is per-code-point dispatch: the parser batches
    printable ASCII into one `writeText` per run and every non-ASCII character
    breaks the run.
    
    BENCHMARKS.md also records the line-recycling experiment this measurement
    suggested, and why it was rejected: blanking an evicted line and pushing it
    back is slower than allocating a new one, because the VM's allocator already
    hands out zeroed typed data.
    
