Author: klc <m_kilic@outlook.com.tr>
Date:   Thu Aug 6 00:11:56 2026 +0300

    test(bench): compare two builds on a pinned grid
    
    The harness measured whatever grid a fixed 1000x600 viewport happened to
    produce, which is not the same grid on two builds: `fix(ui): size cells from a
    reference glyph` moved cell metrics, so that viewport yields 100 columns here
    and 102 on the published package. Comparing those runs credits one build with
    a 2% smaller grid.
    
    It now bisects the viewport extent until the terminal reports exactly the
    requested cell count, and aborts the run rather than reporting numbers taken
    at the wrong one. The grid is settable with BENCH_COLS/BENCH_ROWS, because
    3700 cells is small enough to hide differences that a full-screen 8500-cell
    grid makes obvious.
    
    Arithmetic from the reported cell count does not converge - it only bounds
    cells-per-pixel, and the first attempt oscillated between 37 and 38 rows for
    a dozen resizes. Cell count is monotonic in extent, which is all bisection
    needs.
    
    `script/bench-compare.sh` drives the A/B: the baseline goes in a worktree and
    runs *this* tree's harness, so both sides execute identical measurement code.
    `bench/bench_stats.dart` is the one file that differs - the baseline gets the
    stub, since `TerminalRenderStats` does not exist there.
    
    The macOS window is opened at 1440x920 because it is the ceiling on the grid a
    run can measure, and state restoration has to be disabled or the saved frame
    wins.
    
