Author: klc <m_kilic@outlook.com.tr>
Date:   Mon Aug 3 19:33:01 2026 +0300

    perf(observable): avoid copying listeners on every notification
    
    Terminal.write() calls notifyListeners() on every write, and the previous
    implementation allocated a fresh List via listeners.toList() on every
    single call just to guard against a listener mutating the listener set
    from within its own callback.
    
    Replace the backing Set<void Function()> with an append-only
    List<void Function()?>, tombstoning removed entries (setting the slot to
    null) instead of shifting the list. notifyListeners() then walks the list
    by captured index/length with no allocation in the steady state:
    - Appends during iteration land past the captured length, so they are
      simply not invoked this round (consistent with the old snapshot
      semantics).
    - Removals just null out a slot, which is safe to read as a skip even
      from the middle of the current iteration - no
      ConcurrentModificationError, no need to copy.
    Tombstoned slots are compacted away once they exceed half of a list of at
    least 16 entries, so long-lived Observables with add/remove churn don't
    grow unboundedly.
    
    Also extends test/src/base/observable_test.dart (previously only covered
    self-removal during notification) with cases for adding a listener during
    notification and for a listener that both adds and removes others in the
    same callback.
    
    Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>

