Author: klc <m_kilic@outlook.com.tr>
Date:   Sun Aug 2 16:03:42 2026 +0300

    test(example): add a deterministic render benchmark
    
    Comparing two builds with htop and the DevTools frame chart does not
    work: htop's own load varies run to run, the FPS average conflates the
    UI and raster threads, and debug mode numbers are not comparable to
    anything.
    
    This benchmark drives the terminal from a precomputed byte stream with
    no PTY and no shell, pins the viewport to a fixed logical size so the
    cell count cannot drift between runs, and reports UI and raster
    percentiles separately per workload. Three consecutive profile-mode runs
    on the same machine agree to within 0.3ms.
    
    Five workloads, chosen to separate the code paths:
    
      plain       scrolling ASCII, paragraph cache hits
      sgr         same glyphs, heavy color churn, large cache key space
      boxdraw     box drawing, procedural glyph path, bypasses the cache
      fullscreen  alt-screen full redraw, deterministic stand-in for htop
      flood       32 MiB delivered as fast as the event loop allows, which
                  is the only workload that tests write() throughput rather
                  than paint cost
    
    Frames during the flood are counted with a persistent frame callback
    rather than from FrameTiming: timings are delivered asynchronously and
    in batches, so they undercount a window that has only just closed.
    
    Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>

