Author: klc <m_kilic@outlook.com.tr>
Date:   Sun Aug 2 15:12:53 2026 +0300

    perf(rendering): skip cursor blink repaints that cannot be seen
    
    The cursor blink timer called `markNeedsPaint` unconditionally every
    750ms, and a repaint of this render object redraws the entire viewport -
    roughly 926 `drawParagraph` calls on an 80x24 screen - in order to
    toggle a single rectangle.
    
    The blink phase is only observable under fairly narrow conditions, so
    the timer now checks them before scheduling a frame: the render object
    must be laid out, the cursor must not be pinned visible by
    `alwaysShowCursor` or by an active IME composition, `cursorVisibleMode`
    must be on, and the cursor row must be scrolled into view. Reading back
    through scrollback or running a full-screen program that hides the
    cursor no longer costs a full viewport repaint twice a second.
    
    The 5s blink timeout keeps its repaint, but only when it has to restore
    a cursor that was left in the hidden phase.
    
    Note this is a narrower win than it first appears: `_cursorBlinkTimeout`
    already stops blinking 5s after the last terminal change, so the waste
    was bounded to the seconds following output rather than continuous.
    
    Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>

