    perf(rendering): reuse paint and cell scratch objects in the paint path
    
    Every frame the painter allocated a fresh `Paint` for each cursor,
    highlight, underline, strikethrough, dotted/dashed/wavy decoration and
    frame decoration it drew, plus a `CellData` per line for both the
    background and the foreground pass. On an 80x24 screen the `CellData`
    allocations alone are around 50 per frame, and a screen with underlined
    or hyperlinked text adds one `Paint` per decorated cell on top.
    
    These are all short-lived objects created inside the paint loop, so the
    cost is new-space pressure and more frequent scavenges on the UI thread
    rather than raw allocation time.
    
    The painter now owns reusable `_decorationPaint`, `_highlightPaint` and
    `_cursorPaint` instances alongside the existing foreground/background
    pair, and a single `_scratchCellData` for the line loops. Every call
    site assigns all fields it depends on - including `style`, which
    previously varied between callers - so no state leaks between draws.
    `RenderTerminal` does the same for its per-frame fills and for the
    per-segment underline paint.
    
